{"findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"suppression-governance-bypass","classStatement":"Suppression governance must cover changes to effective suppression scope, including changes that add no directive.","document":"requirements/design.md","evidence":"Lines 84–87 require inline suppressions to have no effect and legacy suppressed findings to enter the baseline. C1.2 (line 199) instead scans only added lines for suppression directives and leaves legacy directives effective. Deleting an existing eslint-enable directive, or adding code inside an existing disabled region, introduces no added suppression directive.","failureScenario":"A branch deletes the closing eslint-enable directive before newly added floating-promise violations -> the added-line scan requests no approval, ESLint suppresses the new violations, and the wrapper reports PASS despite a regression.","id":"F1","severity":"high"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"baseline-cache-scope-mismatch","classStatement":"A cached partial baseline must identify its analyzed file scope or explicitly populate missing baseline entries before comparison.","document":"requirements/design.md","evidence":"Lines 47–48 cache a baseline computed only over changed files, keyed by merge-base commit, governed-config hashes and tool versions. C1.4 (line 205) confirms that the generated files list is the exact changed-file set. The cache key omits that set and its rename mapping.","failureScenario":"One round caches the baseline for changed file A; a later round also touches B without changing the merge base, config or tool versions -> the reused baseline lacks B's existing findings, so unchanged legacy findings in B are reported as regressions and incorrectly block the round.","id":"F2","severity":"medium"}],"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"The proposal retains the mandated default-on receipt, human opt-out, GateReeve wrapper, merge-base comparison, config approval, plugin, doctor and dogfooding. C1 records mechanism amendments. The explicit exclusions and branch prohibition remain intact; nothing grants final-PR merge authority, authorizes the handoff commit or proposes automatic installation.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"FAIL","summary":"The problem, scope and overall approach are coherent, but two blocking defects remain: suppression governance can be bypassed, and the baseline cache omits its scan scope."}